{% sec 1, "Arriving First", "What is latency, and why does it decide who wins?", "Arriving first" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think of the first morning of the January sales. Three kinds of people care about being early. The shop assistant wants to swap old price tags before bargain hunters spot a mistake. The shoppers at the front of the queue get first pick of the best items. And a few professional resellers rush to whichever shop has accidentally left last week's lower prices up, to buy everything before the tags are changed. All three are racing the clock, for different reasons.</p>
    <p>In markets, the clock is {% gl "latency" %}latency{% endgl %}: the total delay between something happening and your order acting on it. Deltashark splits it into three parts: market data takes time to arrive, decisions take time to make, and orders take time to reach the venue. Add them up and you know who acts first. When the right response to an event is obvious to everyone, acting first is most of what matters.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Latency is a sum of delays</span>\[ L = \underbrace{t_{\text{data}}}_{\text{news reaches you}} + \underbrace{t_{\text{decide}}}_{\text{your code reacts}} + \underbrace{t_{\text{order}}}_{\text{order reaches the venue}} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The Three Seats showed one firm's budget for this: \(4 + 3 + 5 + 28 = 40\) microseconds from market data in to order out. A microsecond (μs) is a millionth of a second; light travels only about 300 metres in that time.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Three Things Speed Buys", "What exactly do firms get for being fast?", "Three things speed buys" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Deltashark's framing: speed is best understood through what it buys.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Purchase</th><th>What it means</th><th>Who needs it</th><th>Where we met it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Defence</strong></td><td>Cancel or update your quotes before news makes them {% gl "stale quote" %}stale{% endgl %}. A resting quote is a free option, and its value to attackers grows the longer it stays out of date.</td><td>Market makers</td><td>The Free Option; Setting the Width (the toll \(qm\))</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Priority</strong></td><td>Be first to join a new price level, so you sit at the front of the queue.</td><td>Market makers in large-tick markets</td><td>The Price Grid (what a queue spot is worth)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Offence</strong></td><td>When public news means a price elsewhere is stale (say, an index future moves and a stock's quote lags), take it before it's fixed.</td><td>{% gl "latency arbitrage" %}Latency arbitrageurs{% endgl %}</td><td>Competition, Speed and Edge</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Defence and offence are two sides of the same race: the fastest taker collects the difference, and the slowest quoter pays it. For a market maker, every microsecond cut from requoting directly cuts the adverse selection toll in its {% gl "spread floor" %}spread floor{% endgl %}.</p>
  </div>
    <div class="worked">
      <span class="lbl">Worked example (Deltashark): a latency race, priced</span>
      <h3>News moves fair value \(10\) ticks above a resting offer. Firm A reacts in \(40\) μs, firm B in \(60\) μs. Only the faster order reaches the stale quote before it's cancelled.</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>A: \(\pi_A = 10 \text{ ticks} \times \text{size}\).</li>
        <li>B: \(\pi_B = 0\).</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="answer">Winner takes all: \(20\) μs of advantage captures \(100\%\) of the prize. That explains spending that per-trade arithmetic can't: shaving microseconds is worth big fixed costs when every race pays the whole pot to first place.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="worked">
      <span class="lbl">Worked example (Deltashark): defence, priced</span>
      <h3>Faster requoting cuts a maker's informed toll from \(2.0\) to \(1.4\) ticks per fill, over \(5{,}000\) fills a day. What's the daily saving?</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>Saving per fill: \(2.0 - 1.4 = 0.6\) ticks.</li>
        <li>Per day: \(0.6 \times 5{,}000 = 3{,}000\) ticks.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="answer">At \(\$12.50\) a tick that's \(\$37{,}500\) a day, or over \(\$9\) million a year, which pays for a lot of hardware. Defensive speed is often the bigger business case.</p>
    </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "How Often Races Happen", "Are latency races a rare curiosity or a big part of trading?", "How often races happen" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Races are hard to see in ordinary data, because you only see the winner's trade, not the losers' failed orders. Researchers at the UK's Financial Conduct Authority, Matteo Aquilina and Peter O'Neill, with Eric Budish from the University of Chicago, solved this by using the London Stock Exchange's full message data: 2.2 billion messages for FTSE 350 stocks over 43 trading days in autumn 2015, including every failed attempt. What they found:</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>Races are frequent<span class="why">About one a minute for each FTSE 100 stock.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>Races are extremely fast<span class="why">The most common race lasts 5–10 millionths of a second.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>Races are a big share of trading<span class="why">About 20% of all volume, and roughly a third of price impact and of the effective spread.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>A few firms dominate<span class="why">The top six firms account for over 80% of all race wins and losses.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">5</span><span>Each race is small, the total isn't<span class="why">The average race is worth about half a tick, but together they act like a tax of roughly 0.5 basis points on trading, which the authors scale up to about \(\$5\) billion a year across global stock markets.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Win the race</h3><p>News arrives. A market maker sends a cancel for its now-stale quote, and two snipers send orders to take it. Each firm's reaction time varies a little from race to race ({% gl "jitter" %}jitter{% endgl %}). Whoever's message arrives first wins: if it's the maker's cancel, nobody trades. Add a delay to aggressive orders and see who benefits.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="presets sp-pick" id="wr-pick" aria-label="Race presets"></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="wr-m">Maker's cancel</label><input type="range" id="wr-m" min="5" max="120" step="1" value="45"><output id="wr-m-out" for="wr-m">45 μs</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="wr-a">Sniper A</label><input type="range" id="wr-a" min="5" max="120" step="1" value="30"><output id="wr-a-out" for="wr-a">30 μs</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="wr-b">Sniper B</label><input type="range" id="wr-b" min="5" max="120" step="1" value="38"><output id="wr-b-out" for="wr-b">38 μs</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="wr-j">Jitter</label><input type="range" id="wr-j" min="1" max="20" step="1" value="6"><output id="wr-j-out" for="wr-j">±6 μs</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="wr-d">Delay on takers</label><input type="range" id="wr-d" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="0"><output id="wr-d-out" for="wr-d">0 μs</output></div>
    </div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-wr" viewBox="0 0 640 260" role="img" aria-label="When each firm's message arrives, and the share of races each one wins"></svg>
