  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "The Latency Stack", "How do firms actually get faster?", "The latency stack" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Deltashark points out that the latency stack is physical. Every layer can be bought by anyone willing to pay, and because races are relative, each firm's spending is driven by its rivals' spending: the arms race from Competition, Speed and Edge, where the finish line keeps moving but the prize doesn't grow.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it is</th><th>What it saves</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>{% gl "colocation" %}Colocation{% endgl %}</td><td>Your servers in the exchange's own data centre</td><td>Kilometres of cable become metres: milliseconds to microseconds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{% gl "direct feed" %}Direct feeds{% endgl %}</td><td>Each exchange's raw data, not the slower {% gl "consolidated tape" %}consolidated feed{% endgl %}</td><td>The time it takes the consolidator to gather and republish</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{% gl "microwave network" %}Microwave{% endgl %} and shortwave links</td><td>Radio towers in a straight line between financial centres; shortwave bounces off the atmosphere across oceans</td><td>Light in glass fibre travels about a third slower than through air, and fibre routes bend</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{% gl "kernel bypass" %}Kernel bypass{% endgl %}</td><td>Network cards that hand data straight to the trading program, skipping the operating system</td><td>Microseconds per message</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{% gl "fpga" %}FPGAs{% endgl %}</td><td>Chips programmed to run the trading logic in hardware</td><td>Microseconds become nanoseconds</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The Chicago–New Jersey route shows how this played out. Competition, Speed and Edge told the story of Spread Networks' straight fibre line in 2010, at about 13 milliseconds for a round trip. By 2013 a microwave network from McKay Brothers had cut the round trip to about 8.2 milliseconds, simply because radio through air beats light through glass: air's refractive index is about \(1.0003\), glass fibre's about \(1.5\). Microwave is less reliable (heavy rain can knock links out), so firms use both. Across the Atlantic, where you can't build towers, some firms have tried shortwave radio for the same reason.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 5, "Matching Speed to the Signal", "How fast does your strategy actually need to be?", "Matching speed to the signal" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Not all trading needs the frontier. Deltashark: speed-sensitivity is a property of the strategy. Quoting and short-horizon arbitrage compete in microseconds; execution algorithms in milliseconds to seconds; anything holding for days competes on information and sizing, where being a microsecond early is worth nothing. <strong>The right latency budget is the one matched to how fast the signal you're trading fades.</strong></p>
    <p>One way to picture it: if a signal's edge halves every \(h\) (its {% gl "half-life" %}half-life{% endgl %}), then a delay of \(L\) leaves you \(2^{-L/h}\) of the edge. When \(L\) is far smaller than \(h\), you keep almost everything and more speed buys nothing; when \(L\) is close to \(h\) or bigger, speed is the whole game.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>What does a microsecond buy?</h3><p>Pick a strategy and see how much of its edge survives from five different set-ups, from an FPGA in the exchange's data centre to a person clicking a mouse. The curve shows the share of edge kept at every delay. Note the time axis: each step is ten times longer than the last.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="presets sp-pick" id="ms-pick" aria-label="Strategy"></div>
      <div class="presets sp-pick" id="ms-setup" aria-label="Your set-up"></div>
    </div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-ms" viewBox="0 0 640 260" role="img" aria-label="Share of a strategy's edge kept against latency on a log scale, with five set-ups marked"></svg>
    <div class="legend"><span><i class="sw tone"></i>Edge kept</span><span><i class="sw grey"></i>Each set-up's latency</span></div>
    <div class="readout" id="ms-read"></div>
    <p class="look" id="ms-story"></p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "Designs That Blunt the Race", "If pure speed races waste money, what can venues do about it?", "Designs that blunt the race" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Deltashark's view: the private value of speed is bigger than its value to society, because races consume resources without adding liquidity. That motivates three design experiments, all introduced in Competition, Speed and Edge:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Design</th><th>How it works</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>{% gl "speed bump" %}Speed bump{% endgl %} (sometimes asymmetric)</td><td>A small fixed delay on incoming orders; if only takers are delayed, makers can cancel stale quotes first</td><td>IEX's 350 μs coil; Eurex's {% gl "passive liquidity protection" %}passive liquidity protection{% endgl %}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{% gl "frequent batch auction" %}Frequent batch auctions{% endgl %}</td><td>Orders collected and matched at intervals, so arrival order inside a batch doesn't matter: races on time become competition on price</td><td>Proposed by Budish, Cramton and Shim (2015); the opening and closing auctions most exchanges already run work the same way</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Randomised or batched processing</td><td>Orders held for a short, sometimes random, time, so a tiny speed edge rarely decides the winner</td><td>EBS's {% gl "latency floor" %}latency floor{% endgl %} in FX</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Eurex's version is the clearest recent example. It switched on passive liquidity protection for its FX futures in May 2019, then tested it in French and German equity options (a 3 millisecond delay on aggressive orders in French single-stock options, 1 millisecond in German ones). After encouraging results it added FTSE 100 index options in 2021 and EURO STOXX 50 index options in October 2022. Optiver, a big options market maker, reported that competition at the top of the book and liquidity improved. Each design shifts profit between takers, makers and natural investors, which is why Deltashark calls this one of the liveliest areas of market design.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "The Chapter on One Page", "How do ticks, order flow and speed fit together?", "The chapter on one page" %}
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Idea</th><th>The rule</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>The grid</td><td>If the spread makers need is under one tick, the spread is stuck at one tick and the queue earns the surplus</td><td>The Price Grid</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Changing the grid</td><td>Bigger ticks: deeper queues, wider spreads. Smaller: tighter spreads, thinner size, flicker</td><td>Changing the Tick</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pressure</td><td>Sign trades; \(\text{OFI} = (V_b - V_s)/(V_b + V_s)\); book imbalance moves price with slope \(\propto 1/\text{depth}\)</td><td>Reading the Tape</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Who's behind it</td><td>Size leaves persistent imbalance; information leaves impact that stays (average about two-thirds of the peak)</td><td>Footprints of Size and Information</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Who acts first</td><td>Speed buys defence, priority and offence; match latency to your signal's half-life</td><td>What Speed Buys</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember from the chapter</span>
    <p>Prices move in steps, pushed by order flow, and the people who see and act first collect most of the small edges. Your job is to know which game you're in: the grid, the flow and the clock decide it before any strategy does.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 8, "And in FX, Gold and Bonds?", "Where does speed matter in my three markets?", "FX, gold and bonds" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>FX</h3>
    <p>FX has some of the fiercest races, because the same currency pair trades on many platforms and in London, New York and Tokyo at once. It also has some of the strongest counter-measures: EBS's latency floor, randomised matching on other platforms, Eurex's passive liquidity protection on its FX futures, and {% gl "last look" %}last look{% endgl %}, which lets dealers reject trades on prices that have just gone stale.</p>
    <h3>Gold</h3>
    <p>Gold trades as COMEX futures in Chicago-area data centres, as spot in London, and through ETFs in New York, so price gaps between them are a classic target for speed. ICE proposed a delay for aggressive orders in some of its gold and silver futures around the same time as Eurex's experiment, a sign that exchanges in metals worry about the same thing.</p>
    <h3>Bonds</h3>
    <p>On-the-run Treasuries are a speed market: principal trading firms race between Treasury futures in Chicago and cash Treasuries on the electronic interdealer platforms, which is one reason the Chicago–New Jersey microwave links matter for bonds as much as for stocks. Older Treasuries and corporate bonds trade by request-for-quote, where seconds don't matter and relationships do.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Colo</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Short for colocation: servers in the exchange's data centre.</p><p class="eg">"Our colo box in Aurora is down, we're pulling quotes."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Wire-to-wire</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Latency from market data hitting your network card to your order leaving it.</p><p class="eg">"We're under two micros wire-to-wire on the FPGA."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Micros</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Microseconds.</p><p class="eg">"We lost that race by three micros."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Jitter</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>How much your latency varies from message to message.</p><p class="eg">"Average is fine, it's the jitter that's killing us."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Sniped</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A stale quote taken by a faster trader before you could cancel it.</p><p class="eg">"Got sniped on the open, the future moved first."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Pulled in time</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Cancelled a quote before it could be picked off.</p><p class="eg">"Pulled in time, no fills on the headline."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>The tower</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A microwave link, and by extension the speed edge it gives.</p><p class="eg">"Rain took the tower out, we're on fibre today."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Hot path</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The code that runs between seeing data and sending an order; every nanosecond on it matters.</p><p class="eg">"Keep logging off the hot path."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 10, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where speed turns up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: don't enter races you can't win</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>From home, your latency is tens of milliseconds at best, thousands of times slower than the firms in this note. So pick strategies whose edge lasts minutes to days, where that delay costs almost nothing, and avoid anything that depends on reacting to news or prices first, such as scalping a few ticks around data releases. Prop firm rules often ban latency arbitrage and news trading for the same reason. Your edge has to come from research, patience and sizing, not the clock.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: speed means hearing first</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Property has latency too, measured in days rather than microseconds. The best deals (an off-market villa, a distressed sale) go to whoever hears first and can commit fastest: agents' early calls are the "direct feed", having finance and lawyers ready is the "colocation". The same three purchases apply: defence (re-pricing your listings fast when the market moves), priority (first viewing), and offence (buying a mispriced plot before others notice).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: defence for your quotes</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A naira–dollar app that shows users a rate is a market maker with stale-quote risk. When the official or parallel rate jumps, the users who react first get yesterday's price, and the app pays the toll. The defence is the same as on an exchange: update quoted rates automatically and quickly from reliable feeds, pause or widen when the source rate is moving fast, and consider a short "quote valid for" window, the app's own version of last look, stated clearly to users.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 11, "The Maths Underneath", "How do you put numbers on races, distance and decay?" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Who wins a two-way race, if reaction times are normally spread</span>\[ \begin{gathered} P(A \text{ first}) = \Phi\!\left(\frac{\mu_B - \mu_A}{\sqrt{\sigma_A^{2} + \sigma_B^{2}}}\right) \end{gathered} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>\(\mu\) is each firm's average reaction time, \(\sigma\) its jitter, and \(\Phi\) the standard normal probability from What Quant Trading Is. A small average lead wins almost every time if jitter is low, and much less often if jitter is high: consistency matters as much as raw speed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">How long a signal takes to travel, and how much edge survives</span>\[ \begin{gathered} t = \frac{d \times n}{c} \qquad c \approx 300 \text{ km per ms} \\[6pt] \text{edge kept} = 2^{-L/h} \end{gathered} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>\(d\) is distance, \(n\) the refractive index (about \(1.5\) in fibre, \(1.0003\) in air), \(L\) your latency and \(h\) the signal's half-life.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example</span>
    <h3>The straight-line distance from the CME's data centre near Chicago to the New Jersey stock exchange data centres is roughly \(1{,}190\) km. What's the fastest possible round trip by microwave, and by fibre on the same straight line?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Microwave, one way: \(1{,}190 \times 1.0003 / 300 \approx 3.97\) ms. Round trip: about \(7.9\) ms.</li>
      <li>Fibre on the same line, one way: \(1{,}190 \times 1.5 / 300 \approx 5.95\) ms. Round trip: about \(11.9\) ms.</li>
      <li>Real fibre routes bend, which is why Spread Networks' best was about \(13\) ms, and real microwave links need relay towers, which is why the best are just under \(8\) ms.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Physics sets the floor; the arms race is about getting as close to it as money allows.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 12, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer. These double as interview questions." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>(Deltashark quick check) A stale-quote race pays \(8\) ticks at \(12.50\) per tick on \(10\) contracts, winner-take-all. What does the winner collect?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(8 \times 12.50 \times 10 = \$1{,}000\).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>(Deltashark quick check) Faster requoting cuts a maker's informed toll from \(2.0\) to \(1.4\) ticks per fill over \(5{,}000\) fills a day. What is the daily saving in ticks?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\((2.0 - 1.4) \times 5{,}000 = 3{,}000\) ticks.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>Firm A averages \(30\) μs with \(\pm 4\) μs jitter; firm B averages \(33\) μs with \(\pm 3\) μs. Roughly how often does A win?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(\Phi(3/\sqrt{16 + 9}) = \Phi(0.6) \approx 73\%\). A three-microsecond lead wins about three races in four, not all of them.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>How long does light take to travel \(50\) km through fibre?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(50 \times 1.5 / 300 = 0.25\) ms, or \(250\) μs, about five microseconds per kilometre.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>A signal has a half-life of \(2\) seconds. What share of its edge survives \(50\) ms of latency? And \(1\) second?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(2^{-0.05/2} \approx 98\%\) and \(2^{-1/2} \approx 71\%\).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>Interview question: is high-frequency speed good or bad for ordinary investors?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Both. Defensive speed lets market makers quote tighter and bigger, because they're picked off less; that helps investors. Offensive speed, sniping stale quotes, is a transfer from market makers to the fastest firms, and makers pass that cost on through wider spreads. The FCA study estimated races add up to roughly a 0.5 basis point tax on trading. So the debate is about designs (asymmetric speed bumps, batch auctions) that keep the defensive benefits while reducing the races.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">7</span><span>Interview question: you're building your first trading system on a cloud server. How do you decide whether latency matters?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Estimate how fast my signal decays: measure how much of the expected move happens within a second, a minute, an hour of the signal. Then compare that half-life with my end-to-end latency (tens of milliseconds on a cloud server, plus my broker's). If the half-life is minutes or longer, latency costs almost nothing and I'll spend effort on research and costs instead. If most of the move happens in under a second, I'm competing with firms thousands of times faster, so I'd drop or redesign the strategy rather than try to win that race.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}
